porting late blight in new york state  and by the 17th of august this many  counties had been reporting late blight  in new york state  so i thought i'd tally that you know  just do a cumulative  tally of the number of reports to our  diagnostic lab about late blight  and you can see that the first  event was in  mid-june and here's the report on the  23rd  here's the 24th the 25th  i want and then it goes way up to above  140 reports i want to compare that with  2008 the number of reports of late  blight in new york state so there were  three samples received by our diagnostic  by our diagnostic lab  none of them was late light they were  all suspected so there was zero late  blight in new york imported to our  diagnostic lab in 2008 but in 2009 we  had this um  this kind of reporting  i also thought i'd say that  37 of these 30 or 49 reports that were  occurred over a period of six days were  from retail stores which is was not  normal  and some of our colleagues in the state  from cornell cooperative extension would  go to store find that there was late  light there and suggest to the  manager that you have to get rid of  these plants they're a problem  so what happened was  that  some people  put them on sale to move them out  this is a  this is a picture of an organic farm not  too far from here  i was there in early august 2009  this they had 10 or 11 plantings of  tomatoes  this is either the 10th or 11th planting  it was only planting left they removed  everything else because it had been  affected by late blight  these were going to be destroyed and  moved very quickly because as you can  see they are very severely affected and  that story was repeated many times in  many locations  in 2009  this is a message i got from a lady  growing  tomatoes  in amsterdam new york or near amsterdam  it's just illustrative according to the  impact that this epidemic had  she said i am an organic gardener in  amsterdam new york with 63 heirloom  tomato plants of 23 differ